  "rationale": "Multiple direct-match melanoma PRS are present (e.g., PGS000079, PGS000118, PGS000732, PGS000743, PGS000766, PGS002246/PGS002247, PGS001304, PGS004795/PGS004796). PGS000079 is best supported in the provided context because it targets an incident melanoma endpoint (phenotyping_reported: \"Incident melanoma\"), is evaluated in Europeans, and is supported by a very large validation sample (n=392,803) with consistent effect-size evidence (HR 1.43, 95% CI 1.36–1.49) and discrimination reported (full-model AUROC 0.652; C-index 0.663). Competing melanoma-labeled models either have much smaller validation (e.g., PGS000743 n=1,035 with high full-model AUROC that may be covariate-driven; PGS000813 is familial melanoma in non-carriers of CDKN2A, a more restricted endpoint), are framework-style sparse UKB models (PGS001304 snpnet) which domain guidance cautions often underperform disease-focused scores, or are risk/survivor/screening-context endpoints (e.g., second primary melanoma in PGS003412). Main limitation: PGS000079 does not report PRS-only AUROC/R2, so its full-model AUROC may partially reflect covariates; this lowers certainty versus candidates that would provide PRS-only metrics, but within the visible evidence PGS000079 remains the most coherent, broadly applicable incident melanoma choice."
}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS005219","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"Direct-match candidates are present for sleep apnea, including multiple ExPRSweb “Sleep apnea” models (PGS003204–PGS003218) and multiple “Obstructive sleep apnea” models (PGS003479, PGS003857, PGS003858, PGS005219, PGS005220). Domain knowledge states obstructive sleep apnea is the clinically dominant form of sleep apnea, so OSA-labeled models are acceptable direct matches.

Among OSA candidates, PGS005219 is best supported by the visible evidence: it targets obstructive sleep apnea, uses an explicitly multi-score aggregation approach (“weighted PRSsummation PRS-CSs”) with ~983k variants (a strong structural signal), is developed across multiple cohorts (FinnGen, MGBB, MVP), and reports strong effect size evidence (OR=1.98, 95% CI 1.74–2.24) with a reported AUROC=0.79. Its covariate set includes only standard demographic/ancestry terms plus BMI, which is described in the domain guidance as a mild/standard epidemiologic adjustment.

Main limitation: the reported AUROC is a full-model metric (no PGS-only AUROC/R2 or incremental AUROC shown), so the PRS-specific contribution cannot be cleanly compared across candidates; additionally, other OSA models (e.g., PGS003857/PGS003858) also report high full-model AUROC (~0.77) under BMI-adjusted endpoints, creating meaningful competition without PRS-only metrics to decisively separate them."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS005219","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"Direct-match candidates are present for the target trait (sleep apnea), including multiple generic “Sleep apnea” models (PGS003204–PGS003218) and several clinically dominant subtype models for obstructive sleep apnea (PGS003479, PGS003857, PGS003858, PGS005219, PGS005220). Domain knowledge explicitly states obstructive sleep apnea is the dominant clinical form of sleep apnea, so OSA-labeled scores are acceptable direct matches when better supported.

Among OSA candidates, PGS005219 has the strongest visible PRS-relevant evidence package: it targets obstructive sleep apnea, uses an explicit multi-score/weighted summation approach (“weighted PRSsummation PRS-CSs”, 982,740 variants), reports strong effect size (OR=1.98, 95% CI 1.74–2.24), and shows full-model AUROC=0.79 with a large validation sample (n=21,975) and multi-cohort development cohorts listed (FinnGen, MGBB, MVP). Compared with sibling PGS005220 (same method family, similar AUROC, but weaker OR=1.53), PGS005219 is better supported by effect-size magnitude.

Main limitation: none of the candidates provide explicit PRS-only discrimination metrics (PGS-only AUROC/R2) in the provided context; the high full-model AUROC values (including for PGS005219 and the MVP GWAS-hits scores) may be influenced by covariates (notably BMI is included), so the standalone PRS contribution cannot be cleanly compared across models. This keeps the direct-match recommendation below high-confidence despite clear endpoint alignment and strong OR evidence for PGS005219."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS000611","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"All 20 returned candidates are direct matches for urinary bladder cancer based on trait_reported/trait_efo and generally consistent phenotyping_reported (e.g., “Bladder cancer”, “Cancer of bladder”, “Incident bladder cancer”, “Urothelial carcinoma”). No candidate reports a clearly PRS-only AUROC/R2, so selection must rely on endpoint fidelity plus the most PRS-informative available signals (effect sizes, plus structural coherence).

PGS000611 is preferred because it is endpoint-aligned (“Cancer of bladder”), uses a simple GWAS-hits construction (plausible for bladder cancer per provided domain knowledge noting strong-locus architectures can favor sparse hit-based scores), and shows relatively strong PRS-linked evidence within this set: per-SD OR=1.284 (1.211–1.361) and a strong tail contrast (OR top 1% vs rest = 2.66, 1.79–3.93) along with a modest full-model AUROC (0.567) under basic covariates (age/sex/batch/PCs). It also has a larger development sample than its near-clone GWAS-hits sibling (PGS000610; n=12,992 vs 7,469) and similar performance, making it the better-supported of that coherent PRSweb “GWAS Hits” pair.

Main limitation: the cataloged AUC values for many models (including higher ~0.80 for PGS000071/PGS000782) are full-model and include non-genetic covariates (including smoking/BMI), and there is no incremental/PGS-only discrimination reported for the top contenders; thus cross-model performance comparability is weak and prevents a high-confidence ‘best’ choice."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS000611","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"All 20 candidates are direct matches for urinary bladder cancer by trait_reported/trait_efo and generally diagnosis-aligned phenotyping (e.g., “Bladder cancer”, “Cancer of bladder”, “Incident bladder cancer”, “Urothelial carcinoma”). No model reports clearly PRS-only discrimination (PGS-only AUROC/R2 are null throughout), so ranking must rely on endpoint fidelity, structural signals, and effect sizes rather than full-model AUROC (which is not a valid substitute for PRS-only performance and can be covariate-driven per domain guidance).

Among the candidates with comparable endpoint wording (“Cancer of bladder” / “Bladder cancer”) and light demographic/PC covariates, the Cancer PRSweb ‘GWAS Hits’ models show the strongest per-SD effect sizes: PGS000611 reports OR=1.284 (95% CI 1.211–1.361) and a strong top-1% vs rest OR=2.66 (1.79–3.93) with AUROC=0.567, in a coherent UK Biobank development/evaluation context. This provides stronger PRS signal evidence than many genome-wide shrinkage siblings from the same PRSweb family (AUC ~0.52–0.54; OR per SD ~1.08–1.14) and than other non-PRSweb sparse scores with weaker or less comparable metrics. 

Main limitation: evidence remains incomplete/partly non-comparable across studies because (i) the available AUROCs are full-model and covariate-dependent, (ii) multiple strong-looking incident-cancer models (e.g., PGS000071/PGS000782 with AUROC ~0.80) likely reflect substantial covariate contribution (age/sex/smoking/BMI) rather than isolated PRS performance, and (iii) several candidates are close (e.g., PGS000613 has very similar OR per SD 1.301 and top-1% OR 2.91), making a single ‘best’ choice not definitively established from the visible metadata alone."}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS003381",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "Direct-match candidates for the target umbrella trait (uterine cancer) are present, including exact/near-exact uterine labels (PGS000541, PGS000542, PGS001795, PGS003740) and the clinically dominant subtype endometrial/uterine endometrial carcinoma (PGS000075, PGS000786, PGS002300, PGS002735-002737, PGS003381, PGS004244). Given domain guidance that endometrial carcinoma is the dominant subtype of uterine carcinoma and can be a strong direct match, PGS003381 is preferred because it has high endpoint fidelity (phenotyping_reported: uterine endometrial carcinoma) and a genome-wide construction (lassosum; 529,365 variants) with supportive reported discrimination (full-model AUROC 0.761; R² 0.11) under relatively basic covariates (age, top 20 PCs), which is structurally stronger than small GWAS-hits uterine scores (PGS000541/PGS000542) and avoids the portability/framework penalty applying to the global-biobank framework score (PGS001795). However, confidence is limited because the reported AUROC/R² appear to be full-model metrics (not clearly PRS-only), making cross-model performance comparability uncertain; additionally, multiple endometrial/uterine candidates exist without PRS-only metrics, preventing a clearly unambiguous best choice."
}

